    "created_at": "2020-03-16T17:01:35Z",
    "updated_at": "2020-03-16T17:01:57Z",
    "body": "> I don't think that `requestDevice()` needs to specify the protection level. The UA should be able to attempt to upgrade the connection to a secure one if it fails to connect to a protected characteristic. This process is different on the various OS'es. macOS performs the pairing process automatically, but other platforms require this logic to be implemented in Chrome.\r\n> \r\n> For Chromium specifically, this [issue](https://bugs.chromium.org/p/chromium/issues/detail?id=960258) provides context on what needs to be done there in order to be able to connect to protected characteristics.\r\n\r\nWell I think the idea to only \"automatically upgrade on failure from peripheral\" is broken by design, from a security perspective.\r\n\r\nIt's very sad that Apple has chosen this idea, since it doesn't give the client side any security promises. The client might of course want to assure a notification comes from a trusted device, or that a write goes to a trusted device. Now a client can't make the requirement that the connection must be MITM protected, for example.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
